a structures find  optimal number of clusters explore inter  and intra cluster variation and evaluate  how well clusters match our expectations  if we have labeled data  importantly we can test the results  visually using dimensionality reduction  methods with two or three projections to  help us spawn unexpected variation  we can also use clustering to study the  features  or the objects that we have in our data  for example when we use clustering to  study features in our data we have to  transpose the data frame in our example  we can explore the gene expression  profiles to find closely related  patterns that differentiate between  genes responsible for various biological  functions  in this data frame  our rows are genes and columns are  samples we can transpose the data frame  and look at it in a different way we can  look at  samples being the rows and genes being  the columns  however since the number of genes we  have is much greater than the number of  samples  linear dimensionality reduction gives us  a somewhat meaningless picture that  explains a lot of data variants without  any distinct patterns  which means the patterns might be more  complex than expected with many smaller  groups of genes that are linked together  in such cases non-linear methods like  t-sne or u-map can be useful  non-metric embedding methods like t-sne  and u-map can be used for  high-dimensionality data together with  density clustering like db scan hdb scan  optics and spectral clustering to  identify closely related clusters of  genes that can have a biological  interpretation  here you see a visual of db scan after  dimensionality reduction with t-sne and  umap  importantly umap can provide us with  multiple projections while t-sne is  limited to just a few  knowing how to use different methods and  what are the limitations of some of the  clustering methods we can arrive at an  appropriate combination of  dimensionality reduction and clustering  to identify meaningful groups