les  numbers thresholds and any other  algorithm specific data structures  required to make predictions  once the model is trained it can become  a method that we rely on to deal with  new data or it can be studied to really  understand what's going on inside the  data set and why we find certain  patterns to train the model we have to  label the data by using phenotypic  information like cancer stage subtype or  risk factor  an algorithm learns this target mapping  function from training data  the learning can be summarized as a  learning of a function f that maps input  variables x to output variables y  assumptions about the data and possible  function that models relationships can  greatly simplify the learning process a  learning model that summarizes data with  a set of parameters of fixed size  independent of the number of training  examples is called a parametric model no  matter how much data you throw at a  parametric model it won't change its  mind about how many parameters it needs  non-parametric methods are good when you  have a lot of data and no prior  knowledge and when you don't want to  worry too much about choosing just the  right features in many cases advances in  computational technologies made many  non-parametric methods that were too  costly to train accessible to anyone on  sizable data sets decisions about  assigning a sample to one class or the  other follow a parametric or a  non-parametric approach svm and random  forest are both non-parametric  approaches that rely on data quantity  the output of a model can be evaluated  for accuracy precision recall and other  representations that we went over in  exercises  importantly we saw how for most  non-parametric methods training and test  data that accurately represent the  conditions we have plays a major role in  the quality of a model overall since  many data sets have some  over-representation for various classes  it is important to understand these  metrics in the context of each class for  example if the total number of samples  you have is 10 and one class is only two  samples then an 80 accuracy still is  pretty bad for that one class we can  also see how various non-parametric  models are affected by the number of  features one way we can do this is by  running a dimensionality reduction  method that can retain multiple  projections that are used for training  as we can see here umap already does a  great job at retaining the expected data  structure by maximizing distance between  subtypes of samples in our data set in  one of the exercises we reviewed the  number of umap projections has an impact  on the accuracy of classification for  various models support vector machines  performs best overall  that is because we never reach  a point where the number of projections  is greater than 30. random forest  achieves perfect accuracy at five  dimensions decreasing with more number  of dimensions that are being added  k n seems to perform better with higher  number of dimensions but does not  achieve good accuracy overall what are  some of the conclusions that we should  have about these classification models  non-parametric methods are affected by  data using training and test data sets  noise to signal ratio influences  classification quality for models in  different ways the number of features  and what these features represent  whether they represent noise or actual  signal like in umap or pca projections  is also critical for the quality of the  model reducing the number of dimensions  helps some classification models  dimensionality reduction helps us  understand model quality intuitively and  link it to a biologically meaningful  interpretation one of the useful ways to  study a parametric model is to  understand the relationship between  specific features and class predictions  of a model  in the exercises we reviewed you would  have seen many times how a  dimensionality reduction algorithm like  pca is applied to thousands of genes or  just a select few when thousands of  genes are present many of them could be  considered noise that actually prevents  accurate separation between known groups  so we might only spot the most  significant variance in data in the case  of the cell line data set we only see a  separation into two major groups that  are the most different from each other  classification that looks for separation  between defined classes will learn the  difference between classes based on  coefficients we can take the top feature  importance for models like svm or random  forest  performing pca on select features will  reduce noise thus increasing variance  explained by top principal components  the reduced data set of 15 genes has an  overall variance explained of almost 80  percent  compared to only about 30 for the full  data set these 15 genes were selected  based on a model output which assigns  accuracy and impurity measures to each  gene such measures can help select the  best features that can separate between  classes and optimize classification we  can also view pca loadings that show how  each gene profile contributes to  variance direction of each loading can  be used to understand the way such  feature contributes to separation  between labeled groups in our data the  random forest classification relies on  randomly selected portions of data and  many classification trees majority  voting helps select the optimal  decisions for classification the data  points that repeatedly show increased  accuracy and decreased impurity between  classes can be selected as the top  features that contribute to the model  accuracy overall in scikit-learn these  are termed feature importance based on  mean decrease in impurity and feature  importance based on feature permutation  support vectors are data points that are  closer to the hyperplane and influence  the position and orientation of the  hyperplane using these support vectors  we maximize the margin of the classifier  deleting the support vectors will change  the position of the hyperplane these are  the main points that help us build our  svm in linear svm the resulting  separating plane is the same space as  your input features therefore its  coefficients can be viewed as weights of  the inputs dimensions and we can use  these weights to select top features  that are responsible for model accuracy  now that we've completed this review let  us turn to another important topic for  machine learning parametric  classification and regression so let's  now speak about generalized linear  models  first  let's talk about linear regression  variables are terms we use to define our  data in an experiment  any externally controlled variable  related to data collection is called an  independent variable it is manipulated  by what we intend to study  the data we collect are the dependent  variables  these variables represent the  measurements we take of the conditions  in the experiment  oftentimes we would like to establish a  relationship between changes in  independent variable to establish the  relationship of measured dependent  variables with the cause we control  sometimes this cause can be indirect and  therefore not a causal effect even  though it appears to be so  nevertheless establishing a non-random  relationship between the two  is important  let's say we collect the data on eye  color  sex  age height and weight of people  we hypothesize that some variables here  are related and some are not we also  think there are exceptions to the rule  the age and height or weight are related  but maybe not always relationships  observed in data can show which  variables are related or unrelated and  if related then by how much for example  weight and height seem to be logically  related  are they in this data set if we plot a  trend line we will find that the trend  line goes up the bigger the height  weight is also increasing  but the dots are not too close to the  line some dots seem to be far away from  the line  is there a way to quantify this  relationship we found  to study the relationship we can use a  coefficient of correlation or r squared  this measure shows how well the data in  our data set is explained by the  correlation line drawn through this data  in this case we get an r squared  coefficient of 41  40 is okay but not perfect our  conclusion is that height and weight are  correlated but not perfectly  such relationships can be of several  types positive negative and non-linear  linear regression is a way to further  model such a relationship to determine a  mathematical function explaining the  relationship between a dependent and  independent variable in the data set  if there is a relationship such a  function is predictive and will inform  us how the independent variable will  change if we change the dependent  variable  the goal of regression analysis is to  find the right coefficients of this  formula that minimize predictive errors  based on the data that we have the  regression formula has both the  dependent and independent variables in a  mathematical equation plus a constant  intercept and a coefficient  the dependent variable is related  to the independent variable with  additional considerations a constant  intercept or where the line starts from  the slope of the regression line also  known as the coefficient  to get these variables from data we use  the least squares approach it helps  measure the distance from each dot to a  line finding the line that best fits the  data  simple linear regression is an approach  to model the relationship between a  dependent variable and one independent  variable the linear regression model  assumes a linear relationship between  the input and output variables  if this relationship is present we can  estimate the coefficients required by  the model to make predictions on new  data  same principles can be applied in anova  where variables are factors and factor  regression analysis factor regression  analysis is an example of multiple  linear regression that uses a linear  function to predict the value of a  dependent variable containing the  function for n independent variables  it can also be applied to multiple  independent variables the entire set of  data points can be evaluated for fit to  the regression function to use linear  regression we add another parameter  called e or epsilon which is normally  distributed and centered around the true  regression function  this is a random component which  represents the data fit or distance  between the data points to the  regression function by non-random or  structural components  as a result the fitted model can be used  for prediction of continuous or  categorical variables  for categorical variables we can use the  regression approach to model a  relationship between probability of  category for a given observation and the  continuous data we have  if we imagine having just one type of  variable for example bmi  we can use the label  of each observation some clinical  diagnosis for an individual and data x  in our case bmi to model a relationship  between these two variables using a  distribution of samples around two  centers let's call them n for normal and  d for diabetes a gaussian curve of  probable distribution for the given set  of data has to be modeled using an  assumed distribution  its center on the axis and the sigma or  how wide it is  in statistics maximum likelihood  estimation is a method of estimating the  parameters of an assumed probability  distribution given some observed data  we have to determine the distribution  curve of probability for samples with  each label or find the sigma and center  for each gaussian curve  for linear regression we can use the  least squares methods to determine the  most fitted linear regression function  for the model parameters or find the  separation point where the relationship  between probabilities is equal to one  for example if the group of people in a  data set with low bmi is less likely to  have diabetes and the group of people  with high bmi is more likely to have  diabetes we can calculate probability  for each group to have patients with  diabetes and learn the relationship  between bmi and diabetes in terms of  probability  using such a model we can predict which  class any new observation belongs to  quality of our prediction will be  determined by the fit of our model to  data  that is where we need to be mindful of  the assumptions in our parametric model  the probability curve is a sigmoid that  can be modeled by a logistic function  the curve can be used to make a decision  about any data point we might want to do  prediction on the same can be done for  more than one variable for example with  for example with x1 as bmi and x2 as  blood glucose  same as with one variable we will now  have to estimate a probability curve  based on some distribution where the  gaussian multivariate distribution is  used using the same set of linear  equations we can now fit the model to  our data  for any new points we use the data  coefficients to identify the most likely  class it belongs to given data x1 or its  bmi and x2 or its blood glucose this  model for probability is called logistic  regression which is a statistical method  that can also be termed machine learning  in its algorithmic form the goal is to  model the probability of a random  variable y y being 1 or 0 given  experimental data regression modeled  using the logistic function can be  transformed to linear regression to a  logarithm on both sides of the equation  which is also called log transformation  how do we actually make a decision for a  test instance x we say yes if the  probability is more than 0.5 and no if  it is otherwise we call 0.5 the decision  boundary  in scikit-learn logistic regression is a  part of the linear model library that is  because logistic regression uses a  linear function where function  coefficients have a linear relationship  to use the model we need to specify  whether we have a binary problem or a  multi-class problem where we can use one  versus the rest scheme or the  multinomial scheme where multinomial  refers to multi-class we can also  specify the solver for this model for  example lbfgs stands for limited memory  bfgs the breydon fletcher goldfarb  channel algorithm  the solver  is an algorithm to find the optimal fit  in this case applied to gradient descent  gradient descent is a numerical  approximation of the ordinary least  square solution  ordinary least squares is the analytical  solution for a linear regression problem  which finds the least value of squares  of the air  applying this to linear regression is  fairly easy because of found  coefficients and linear equations  gradient descent is an alternative to  the oles solution by iterative method  the basic idea of gradient descent is to  find such a portion of the error curve  using a step which can help us find the  minimum value assuming that the curve is  non-linear for each step where step size  can be set by the user we can  approximate the curve as a straight line  and calculate the descent in weight  until we reach the global optimum for  this approximation  such a solution to maximize fit or  minimize errors in fit for our data can  be applied to generalized linear models  and even non-linear classifiers like  support vector machines  generalized linear models are called  generalized because they assume a  distribution as the basis of their link  function  most commonly used ones are the linear  logistic and poisson regression models  to summarize the glm consists of three  components  the random component which specifies the  conditional distribution of the response  variable y  given the values of the explanatory  variables x  the probability distribution which  generates the observed variable y  the systematic component which specifies  the linear combination that explanatory  variables x are fit into by estimating b  in various ways this is also called the  linear predictor a linear combination of  parameter b and the explanatory variable  x finally the link function which  specifies the link between random and  systematic components link function  literally links the linear predictor and  the parameter for probability  distribution linearity means y is a  linear function of parameter b  regression is a powerful method for both  classification and regression problems  it can be used for continuous variables  or for categorical variables we would  like to predict  categorical prediction can yield weights  for observed variables and independent  observations this is a typical case for  anova or factor regression analysis  where we organize our observations into  groups based on some factor and find a  probability for each variable in  relationship to an effect of that factor  in classification the categorical value  is predicted based on the distribution  of probabilities modeled using a model  that was fit to our data  this model can learn from a given data  set and predict whether a new data set  can be classified a model like that is  called a classifier  for continuous variables regression  allows us to learn from data and predict  the next variable by learning about data  structures  this is often useful when we have  missing data in a set of continuous  variables or in time series data where  previous events can tell us something  about the future this type of a model is  called a regressor  that's all for today in our next session  we'll be speaking about network analysis
 

